foreign  [Music]  for Tuesday January 10th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  so many tech companies have announced  layoffs in recent months last week  Amazon said its planned Cuts would  affect 18 000 workers mostly in its  corporate ranks and Salesforce said it  would trim 10 of its staff Cuts have  also been announced by Facebook parent  meta Twitter snap online real estate  firm Redfin video company Vimeo Uber  Lyft Netflix the list goes on and on  it's a big reversal for a sector that  was hiring quickly during the pandemic  and to be fair many of the laid off  workers are finding new jobs quickly and  unemployment across the U.S remains low  but the cuts take it all together May  signal a shift in how tech companies set  their priorities going forward with us  to discuss this change is our reporter  Dana mattioli hi Dana thanks for being  here anytime so before we get into what  all of this means for the tech sector  can you take us back a bit what is Tech  hiring been like over over the last few  years  over the last few years Tech hiring has  been a frenzy it's been  a major engine of job growth in the  country and these tech companies that  are flush with cash had been throwing  these very lucrative pay packages at  employees and engineers and really  bidding up the market like really very  expensive pay packages with stock  options and the like so it's almost like  a lot of them had this carte blanche  when it came to hiring  all right but now there are all these  layoffs that are happening what are some  of the factors that have led to this  reversal  over the last few months Tech CEOs have  become much more cautious so there's  been chatter of a potential recession on  the horizon there's talk of battening  down the hatches and you know their  clients are starting to slow their  spending and their clients are in Tech  but also other Industries and they're  trying to shore up their balance sheets  right so they're seeing some  cautiousness from their own clients  maybe pulling back spending and also  they're having to answer to their  investors in a different way for a very  long time the tech sector has been  growth above everything that's in their  mindset right grow at all costs and now  the tone is changing where these CEOs  are talking more about profits and  they're getting pressure from either  their Venture capitalists that are  backing them or if they're public  they're public investors that want to  see free cash flow and good balance  sheets some of the passes that they've  gotten historically it seems to have  dried up  what are the CEOs and top Executives at  companies then saying about having to  make these cuts are they taking any  responsibility for example for maybe  hiring too much in the past they are and  I think it's important to get into what  covid was like for a lot of these  companies right so covid sets in and  companies like Amazon and Salesforce and  you know even Facebook saw unprecedented  demand for their products and services  people were home people were online more  so their business lines were just flush  with people bombarding it sometimes at  like record numbers right so they had to  really hire to answer that demand but  there was a gnawing feeling in the back  of a lot of their minds that the party  would stop at some point but they didn't  want to miss out on those revenues in  the interim and now what you're seeing  is as each company announces their  layoffs there's like this CEO Mia culpa  where they're saying sorry we added too  quickly we added too much we have to  sort of right size our businesses so  we're seeing that across the board we're  talking about a lot of big tech  companies is this also affecting smaller  ones in startups as well it is it's  affecting both um I think on the startup  side the VCS are starting to get a  little bit nervous about company cost  being out of whack and they really want  their portfolio companies to shore up  their finances and work more on  profitability than growth so we're  seeing that both in the public companies  but also at the startup side we have a  quote from Excel which is a prominent  venture capitalist saying the bull  market in 2022 was in humility that  these CEOs that you know I think  sometimes are characterized as arrogant  in or Brash how to be more humble in how  they're approaching this  Elon has had some of the biggest and  most notable layoffs what has Andy jassy  the company's CEO said about those yeah  so I sat down with Andy jassy last month  to talk through some of this and a few  things he said so one of the areas that  there have been cuts at Amazon are the  devices or the Alexa business which was  Jeff bezos's baby this was an area that  was high growth and very expensive and  they've had to make some tough decisions  there and he said that he still believes  in that device's business but he said at  the same time there's a number of  experiments that they were running in  that area that just looked like they  weren't going to move the needle enough  and it wouldn't justify the cost that  they were spending on that area and what  about companies like say Redfin one that  has already had layoffs and then had to  do another round of layoffs after that  what did they say about having to do  this twice  yeah so uh their CEO Glenn Coleman he  has some regrets about the way this went  down but they added all these Realtors  and they went into different business  lines during covid and I think the big  lesson he said that he learned was the  simplest way to build a profitable  company isn't to come up with amazing  new ideas to Delight customers he says  it's to stop doing stupid stuff right so  I think if he were to go back he  wouldn't have expanded into different  business lines he would just like focus  on what he does well execute and not get  distracted you're mentioning that both  Jazzy and kelman are mentioning pulling  back from business lines that were maybe  a little more experimental or were off  their essential beat is that what more  tech companies are going to have to do  are they just focusing up on their Core  Business going forward I think it might  be a little too soon to tell we did see  you know Snapchat also pull back from  some of its more moonshot sorts of  projects because you know these  moonshots that Silicon Valley is known  for are expensive they're big bets that  oftentimes don't make it so I wouldn't  be surprised in the coming months or a  year those you know aren't as  prioritized as they would have been in  the past what is this going to mean then  for the tech sector going forward you  know it's tough to say I wouldn't be  surprised if we saw a few quarters of  austerity where these companies that are  you know sort of synonymous with perks  and lavishness and big pay packages  start looking a little bit more like  traditional public companies that have  to answer to Wall Street and investors  it's too soon to say if that sticks  right I guess time will tell but in the  interim it seems like these companies  are going to start behaving like more  Normal public companies all right that's  our reporter Dana mattioli thanks for  joining us Dana anytime and that's it  for today's Tech news briefing for more  Tech stories check out our website  wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall  Street Journal thanks for listening

